% holding-decision-note: Held as a link only (README, "Cultures that protect their music"): the guide's terms cover learning and performing, not copying the score here.
% holding-blocked: Still needed: the composer's written permission before any notation, words or audio are posted on the site (the guide allows learning, performing and sharing for education, not reposting); and a review by the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre or by the composer of the credit, the story and how the class sings it. Neither has happened. Until then the class learns it from the CBC and MusiCounts pages.
% holding-kind: link
% holding-kind-note: No notes or words by design: the composer, CBC Music and MusiCounts share the song for teaching on their own pages, under terms that allow learning, performing and sharing for education but not reposting. Link to their pages.
% source-url: https://www.cbc.ca/music/events/canadian-music-class-challenge/leela-gilday-canadian-music-class-challenge-2023-1.6939014 CBC Music: how Leela Gilday wrote the song, with sheet music and the teaching video (not read here; CBC refuses automated readers)
% source-url: https://musicounts.s3.amazonaws.com/documents/MusiCounts_Singing_In_Indigenous_Languages_final.pdf MusiCounts and CBC Music, Singing in Indigenous Languages (2024), pp. 5-6: the song and the terms
% performance-url: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nw6dSKZSg2A Leela Gilday teaches how to sing "Máhsi ts'enįwę" (CBC Music Class, 2023)
% dossier-function: The 2023 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge Indigenous-language song: a counting song that teaches students to count to five in Dene Kede. The guide says Gilday "talks about using music as a tool for language learning, and hopes that this song will make Dene Kede more approachable for learners of all ages." Search summaries of the CBC article say it was her first song for children, written with her cousin Gloria Gaudet, a kindergarten teacher fluent in the language (to confirm on the CBC page).
% dossier-elements: Not transcribed here; read the key, range and form from CBC's sheet music. A counting song, one to five.
% dossier-practice: Learn the Dene words from Leela Gilday's teaching video. The counting makes it a fit for the youngest grades.
% dossier-accompaniment: The guide (p. 6) asks that traditional Indigenous instruments be left to Indigenous people: "Our instruments are considered sacred" and, if an educator wishes to include them, "it is highly recommended to reserve that role for an Indigenous person, whether that be a student that is knowledgeable on the matter or bringing in a Knowledge Keeper from your community." The Band plays nothing for this record; no hand drum, rattle or powwow drum from us.
% dossier-lyrics: The words are the songwriters', in Dene Kede; not copied here. Pronunciation from the composer's video.
% dossier-honesty: Composed and owned: a 2023 song by a living Dene songwriter, not traditional. Nothing here is ours.
% dossier-licence: Te Credit wording to use: "Máhsi ts'enįwę" by Leela Gilday, with Gloria Gaudet; the 2023 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge Indigenous-language song (CBC Music and MusiCounts) (to confirm with the songwriters).
% dossier-respect: A contemporary teaching song shared for classes. The guide notes Gilday "incorporates traditional Dene drumming in her music"; that drumming is hers and her community's, so the class does not add a Dene drum. Credit the songwriters every time.
% dossier-review: Leela Gilday for the credit and any posting; the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre for classroom use. Neither has reviewed it.
